  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 15,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 148.74, 571.353, 193.231, 585.143 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "Times-Bold",
    "font size" : 11.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Abstract"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 57.6, 480.755, 284.371, 563.645 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The paper deals with the problem of automatic speaker change detection. A metric-based algorithm, called MDISTBIC, which means Modiﬁed DISTBIC, is proposed in this paper. The algorithm originates from the DISTBIC algorithm and modiﬁes it in order to reach a higher efﬁciency. Both the DISTBIC and the MDISTBIC methods are tested in a number of experiments. As the results show, the MDISTBIC algorithm is more efﬁcient than the DISTBIC algorithm in a majority of tests."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 17,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 130.48, 455.953, 211.497, 469.743 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "Times-Bold",
    "font size" : 11.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1. Introduction"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 57.6, 398.845, 284.371, 450.505 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The aim of automatic speaker change detection is to extract homogeneous segments containing the longest possible utterances produced by a single speaker. Many efforts have been devoted to this problem in the last years, mainly due to the large number of possible applications, e.g.:"
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 19,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.92, 193.215, 284.371, 392.755 ],
    "numbering style" : "unordered",
    "number of list items" : 3,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.92, 341.105, 284.371, 392.755 ],
      "font" : "CMSY9",
      "font size" : 8.97,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "• a reliable speaker change detection algorithm could be very helpful for annotators who manually annotate a large amount of speech data in order to organize an archive of audio documents (e.g. recordings of various sessions or meetings, broadcast news, etc.);",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.92, 277.565, 284.371, 339.635 ],
      "font" : "CMSY9",
      "font size" : 8.97,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "• performance of a speech recognition system that recognizes conversations or news broadcasts could be improved, when a speaker change detection system would detect the change of the speaker and parameters of the speech recognition system would be adapted accordingly;",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.92, 193.215, 284.371, 276.095 ],
      "font" : "CMSY9",
      "font size" : 8.97,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "• in speaker recognition systems it is supposed that the input speech belongs only to one speaker. It may cause problems in many real situations (e.g. in conversations or broadcast news), where the speech stream is continuous and there is no information about the beginning and ending of the speech segment of one speaker. A speaker change detection system could help to eliminate such problems.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 57.6, 49.915, 284.376, 184.855 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "There are three main speaker change detection approaches [1], [2]. In the metric-based approach the speaker changes are determined as the moments in which a distance measure computed between two adjacent windows shifted along the speech signal reaches a local maximum. In the model-based approach it is assumed that a model of each speaker the voice of which is contained in an utterance has been trained before the speaker change detection algorithm starts. The speaker changes are then detected as the instants when it is necessary to change the speaker model in order it match the speech signal. The last approach is the decoder-guided approach. Here, the speaker changes are determined according to information provided by a speech recognition system which decodes the spoken audio"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 561.595, 539.491, 582.015 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "stream at ﬁrst (e.g. possible speaker changes are at every silence location)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 467.895, 539.496, 561.195 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this paper, we are interested in the metric-based approach, because it does not require any other information or things except the speech signal itself (i.e. neither speaker model, nor speech recognizer). We focus on the DISTBIC algorithm introduced in [3]. This algorithm is efﬁcient in detecting speaker changes that are relatively close one another, however at the price that a lot of false speaker changes is detected. We will try to modify the algorithm in this paper and thereby to improve its efﬁciency."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 415.835, 539.496, 467.495 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The paper is organized as follows: In Section 2 the DISTBIC algorithm is brieﬂy described. Next, in Section 3 the modiﬁcations of the DISTBIC algorithm are introduced. Experiments are described and their results are presented in Section 4. Finally, a conclusion is given in Section 5."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 24,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 366.72, 391.523, 485.495, 405.313 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "Times-Bold",
    "font size" : 11.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2. DISTBIC algorithm"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 25,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 345.325, 539.491, 386.575 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The DISTBIC algorithm is based on a two-step analysis [3]: the ﬁrst pass uses a distance computation to determine the speaker changes candidates and the second pass uses Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) to validate or discard these candidates."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 26,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 314.095, 539.491, 334.847 ],
    "font" : "Times-Bold",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2.1. First step: detection of speaker change candidate points"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 27,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 225.605, 539.491, 308.485 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The ﬁrst step relies on a distance-based segmentation deﬁned from the likelihoods of adjacent windows. In each window, the data are assumed to result from a single multi-dimensional Gaussian process. The question is, whether the data from the two adjacent windows together ﬁt better with a single multidimensional Gaussian or whether a two-window representation justiﬁes the data better. In order to answer this question, the Kullback-Leibler distance can be used for example."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 28,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 312.72, 183.425, 539.496, 225.205 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.97,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "A symmetric Kullback-Leibler distance KL2 between a vector X coming from the multi-dimensional Gaussian process N(µX, ΣX) and a vector Y resulting from the multidimensional Gaussian process N(µY , ΣY ) can be computed as"
  }, {
